{"id":"layer-norm","t":"Layer normalization (forward)","tags":["Neural Networks","Transformers"],"d":"Easy","rt":"numpy","fn":"layer_norm","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":[],"st":"LayerNorm normalises each token's feature vector to zero mean and unit variance, then applies a learned scale $\\gamma$ and shift $\\beta$:\n\n$$y = \\gamma \\odot \\frac{x - \\mu}{\\sqrt{\\sigma^2 + \\epsilon}} + \\beta, \\qquad \\mu = \\tfrac{1}{d}\\textstyle\\sum_i x_i,\\quad \\sigma^2 = \\tfrac{1}{d}\\sum_i (x_i - \\mu)^2$$\n\nWrite `layer_norm(x, gamma, beta, eps=1e-5)`.\n\n## Requirements\n- `x` has shape `(..., d)`. Normalise over the **last** axis only. `gamma` and `beta` have shape `(d,)`.\n- Use the population variance (divide by $d$, not $d-1$), as frameworks do.\n- Same output shape as `x`.","starter":"import numpy as np\n\ndef layer_norm(x, gamma, beta, eps=1e-5):\n    \"\"\"x: (..., d); gamma, beta: (d,). Normalise over the last axis.\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n","hints":["Reduce over `axis=-1` with `keepdims=True`, so the mean and variance broadcast back over the features.","`np.var` defaults to `ddof=0`, which is the population variance this asks for.","`gamma` and `beta` have shape `(d,)`, which broadcasts against `(..., d)` with no reshaping."],"sol":"import numpy as np\n\ndef layer_norm(x, gamma, beta, eps=1e-5):\n    x = np.asarray(x, dtype=float)\n    mu = x.mean(axis=-1, keepdims=True)\n    var = x.var(axis=-1, keepdims=True)          # ddof=0: population variance\n    return gamma * (x - mu) / np.sqrt(var + eps) + beta\n","ex_md":"## LayerNorm vs BatchNorm\nBatchNorm normalises each feature **across the batch**, so it depends on batch statistics and behaves differently in training and at inference. LayerNorm normalises each sample **across its features**. It works with batch size 1 and variable-length sequences, which is why Transformers use it.\n\n## The role of $\\epsilon$\nA constant row (test 2) has $\\sigma^2 = 0$. With $\\epsilon$ the output is $\\beta$ instead of $0/0$. For tiny-variance rows (last test), $\\epsilon$ visibly damps the normalisation.\n\n## RMSNorm\nLLaMA-style models drop the mean subtraction and $\\beta$: $y = \\gamma \\odot x / \\sqrt{\\overline{x^2} + \\epsilon}$. It's cheaper and works as well in practice.\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- Pre-norm vs post-norm Transformers: why does pre-norm train more stably at depth?\n- Write the backward pass: what is $\\partial L / \\partial x$ given $\\partial L / \\partial y$?","nex":2,"shown":[{"in":"array([[1., 2., 3.]])\narray([1., 1., 1.])\narray([0., 0., 0.])","out":"array([[-1.22473569,  0.        ,  1.22473569]])"},{"in":"array([[5., 5., 5., 5.]])\narray([1., 1., 1., 1.])\narray([0., 0., 0., 0.])","out":"array([[0., 0., 0., 0.]])"}],"lessons":[{"href":"/self-learning-tracks/courses/inference-eng-course/triton-kernel-engineering/write-softmax-rmsnorm/","t":"Write Softmax & RMSNorm","c":"Inference Engineering"},{"href":"/self-learning-tracks/courses/deep-learning/training-deep-networks/layer-and-rms-normalisation/","t":"Layer and RMS normalisation","c":"Deep Learning"}],"related":["scaled-dot-product-attention"],"ntests":6,"tj":"[{\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[1.0, 2.0, 3.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [1.0, 1.0, 1.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [0.0, 0.0, 0.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}], \"e\": {\"__nd__\": [[-1.2247356859083902, 0.0, 1.2247356859083902]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[5.0, 5.0, 5.0, 5.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [1.0, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}], \"e\": {\"__nd__\": [[0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[-4.0283693598287655, 6.428499070790021, -12.56345427042233, 10.561048775566661, 15.434530226920893, -0.52611003007899], [8.304857455543091, 6.509325690841821, 0.05647944541106398, -4.624048655156082, -16.32552427456725, 18.104234840116703], [3.5236776280766606, 29.223274107494348,